From 5676c0c77a0866c4df86dd769db61227e3e430c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gus bourg <gus@bourg.net>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 20:02:13 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 105/145] media: meson: vdec: MPEG-4 Part 2 and H.263 decode on
 G12A/G12B

Add codec_mpeg4, driving the vendor's legacy free-running microcode on
VDEC_1, and offer V4L2_PIX_FMT_MPEG4 and V4L2_PIX_FMT_H263 on G12A/G12B.

MPEG-4 was previously believed to be unsupportable here: bringing it up
wedged the SoC hard - no oops, nothing on the console, only a physical
reset - even when the esparser had rejected the input and not one byte of
bitstream had reached the microcode.  The conclusion drawn at the time was
that the blob must be a "multi" microcode needing a per-frame host
handshake this driver does not implement.

That was wrong, and the real cause is worth stating because it is not
guessable from the register list.  This microcode addresses its DC/AC
prediction, MV and CBP scratch buffers at a FIXED physical base,
DCAC_BUFF_START_IP (0x02b00000), plus an offset it is handed in
AV_SCRATCH_F.  Leaving AV_SCRATCH_F unwritten does not disable that - it
aims the microcode at physical 0x02b00000, which on this SoC is ordinary
kernel memory (00000000-04ffffff : System RAM).  The microcode initialises
that region at start, before it needs any bitstream, so codec start alone
is a bus-master write into the running kernel.  That is the wedge.

Two further things the MPEG1/2 layout gets wrong for this codec:

  - The eight decode buffers are described by canvas triplets in
    AV_SCRATCH_0..3 and AV_SCRATCH_G..J, which are not contiguous.  Writing
    eight consecutive entries from AV_SCRATCH_0 - what MPEG1/2 does - lands
    on AV_SCRATCH_7, which is MP4_PIC_WH, and leaves buffers 4..7 pointing
    at canvas 0.

  - Buffers are recycled by writing the complement of a one-hot mask to
    MREG_BUFFERIN, not the 1-based index MPEG1/2 uses.

The geometry needs the same care.  vdec_s_fmt() assigns sess->width/height
from whatever the CAPTURE S_FMT carried, and a client that sets CAPTURE
before it knows the coded size - ffmpeg does, with a 256x144 placeholder -
leaves that behind.  MPEG1/2 recovers via its sequence-header parse; MPEG-4
had no equivalent, so the placeholder went into MP4_PIC_WH and the canvas
sizes while the microcode decoded at the real size.  Parse the
video_object_layer_header instead, so the stream describes itself.

Tested on a Khadas VIM3 (A311D, G12B), 1280x720 Xvid in AVI: 24/24 frames,
PSNR y 64.83 dB / average 65.90 dB / min 62.78 dB against ffmpeg's software
decoder.  Bit-exactness is deliberately not the gate - MPEG-4 Part 2 does
not specify a bit-exact IDCT, so a conforming hardware IDCT differs from
software within IEEE 1180 limits.  H.264, HEVC, MPEG-1 and MPEG-2 all still
decode 24/24 with the change in place.

The "multi" blobs (gxl_xvid_multi, sm1_xvid_multi) were tried too: they
emit one frame and then wait forever for the per-frame handshake, which
confirms the original reading of that protocol - it was only the
identification of the shipped blob that was wrong.  They do not wedge the
SoC, and the decoder recovers fully afterwards.

Signed-off-by: Gus Bourg <gus@bourg.net>
